==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|la|arguendō}}.

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} for the sake of [[argument]]; [[assume|assuming]] without [[decide|deciding]]; identifying the premises of a hypothetical argument while making it clear that no finding is being made on whether the premises are true.
#* '''1978''', [http://scholar.google.com/scholar_case?case=7635303895390583824&hl=en&as_sdt=2002 ''Matter of Green'', 586 F. 2d 1247 - U.S. Court of Appeals, 8th Circuit]
#*: Assuming '''arguendo''' that those assertions are factually true, we find respondent's claim to be without legal merit.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=William Safire|title=Staying a Superpower|work=The New York Times|date=1990-02-05|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1990/02/05/opinion/essay-staying-a-superpower.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Grant me, '''arguendo''', that sort of scenario, with the Soviet federation rebuilding and small wars raging here and there, as the world of the millennium.}}
# {{lb|en|legal}} {{non-gloss definition|Used to set off the facts presented in an argument on a point of law from facts in dispute in the case.}}

====Usage notes====
* Used after the word it modifies, due to the influence of Latin grammar: ''assuming arguendo'' not *''arguendo assuming''.

